{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Thus says HaShem: By this you shall know that I am HaShem.</b> This expression is stated at the first plague of the series דְּצַ״ךְ, and likewise at the first plague of the series עֲדַ״שׁ it is stated: ״לְמַעַן תֵּדַע כִּי אֲנִי ה׳ בְּקֶרֶב הָאָרֶץ״ (Exodus 8:18), and likewise at the first plague of the series בְּאַחַ״ב it is stated: ״בַּעֲבוּר תֵּדַע כִּי אֵין כָּמֹנִי בְּכָל הָאָרֶץ״ (Exodus 9:14). Mahari'a also raised this difficulty and explained that Pharaoh disputed three things: the first is the existence of HaShem, blessed be He, for he denied HaShem, as it says וַיֹּאמֶר לֹא הוּא (Jeremiah 5:12), and ״לֹא יָדַעְתִּי אֶת ה׳״ (Exodus 5:2). Therefore at the first plague it is stated ״בְּזֹאת תֵּדַע כִּי אֲנִי ה׳״. The second: he would concede, if compelled to admit that there is a deity in existence, nevertheless it does not supervise the lowly. Concerning this it is stated ״כִּי אֲנִי ה׳ בְּקֶרֶב הָאָרֶץ״. The third is that he disputed the power of HaShem, blessed be He, saying that He cannot change nature at all. Concerning this He said ״כִּי אֵין כָּמֹנִי בְּכָל הָאָרֶץ״ — that is to say, He can act as He wills." "And I say to add an explanation to his words, to bring all three plagues under one heading: for the three first plagues came to verify the existence of HaShem, blessed be He, because the Egyptians said that the Nile was their god and there is none besides it. Therefore HaShem executed judgments upon their god, to show that there is One higher above all that is high. And so it is concluded in Bereshit Rabbah (89:4): ״הָרְשָׁעִים מִתְקַיְּמִים עַל אֱלֹהֵיהֶם״ — as it says ״וְהִנֵּה עוֹמֵד עַל הַיְאֹר״ (Exodus 7:15): hear from this that they considered it a deity. Therefore at the plague of the Nile it is stated ״בְּזֹאת תֵּדַע כִּי אֲנִי ה׳״ (Exodus 7:17). And concerning the matter of the frogs that the Nile swarmed — it showed that even the Nile would swarm things that verify the existence of HaShem, blessed be He, for Pharaoh would profane the Name and say ״לֹא יָדַעְתִּי אֶת ה׳״ (Exodus 5:2). Therefore the Nile made known that it is fitting to sanctify the Name, for the frogs sanctified the Name in public, as is found (Pesachim 53b) — that Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah surrendered their lives for the sanctification of the Name, and they learned an a fortiori argument from the frogs that went up into the ovens, and therefore those in the ovens did not die, as is concluded in the Yalkut on this parashah. And concerning the lice it is stated ״אֶצְבַּע אֱלֹהִים הִוא״ (Exodus 8:15). See that in this plague even the sorcerers acknowledged and proclaimed the sovereignty of His Name, blessed be He — to say that this plague was from Him, blessed be He, and the admission of a litigant is like a hundred witnesses." "And in the series עֲדַ״שׁ it is hinted that He, blessed be He, supervises below in all the details of each individual person — to remove from the heart of those who say that the Holy One, blessed be He, supervises only upon the generality of the species and not in particularity upon all the ways of a person. And according to their words, everything comes in a jumble, for there is no distinction between one person and another, and between one who serves Elohim and one who does not serve Him. Therefore the swarm (עָרֹב) came upon them in a jumble (עִרְבּוּבְיָא), and it is written ״וְהִפְלֵיתִי בַיּוֹם הַהוּא אֶת אֶרֶץ גֹּשֶׁן אֲשֶׁר עַמִּי עֹמֵד עָלֶיהָ לְבִלְתִּי הֱיוֹת שָׁם עָרֹב״ (Exodus 8:18), and this is a decisive sign that there is a distinction between righteous and wicked and that the Holy One, blessed be He, supervises over all the details of each person. Therefore it is stated there: ״לְמַעַן תֵּדַע כִּי אֲנִי ה׳ בְּקֶרֶב הָאָרֶץ״ (Exodus 8:18) — to say that His supervision is particular even among the lower beings. And likewise in the plague of pestilence it is written ״וְהִפְלָה ה׳ בֵּין מִקְנֵה יִשְׂרָאֵל וּבֵין מִקְנֵה מִצְרָיִם״ (Exodus 9:4), and that is from the aspect of His supervision, blessed be He, in particularity. And likewise with the boils — the dust flew throughout all the land of Egypt, and even so He distinguished between Israel and Egypt. Therefore the sorcerers could not stand before Moses — that is to say, they could not hold their ground in the argument they had with Moses because of the boils. For every plague they would give an explanation, except this one for which they could not give an explanation: for all the plagues came at one stroke upon all Egypt, and the sorcerers were included in the plague; but the plague of boils first came upon the sorcerers and afterward upon all Egypt, as it says ״כִּי הָיָה הַשְּׁחִין בַּחַרְטֻמִּים וּבְכָל מִצְרָיִם״ (Exodus 9:11). Therefore they could not stand in argument, for Moses charged against them that their own sin caused them to be stricken first — for until now the sorcerers had been causing the hardening of Pharaoh's heart through their enchantments and their lies." "In the series בְּאַחַ״ב, Mahari'a explained that they came to verify the power of HaShem, blessed be He. And it appears to me that Pharaoh claimed there are two authorities; therefore at the first plague of the series בְּאַחַ״ב it is stated ״לְמַעַן תֵּדַע כִּי אֵין כָּמֹנִי בְּכָל הָאָרֶץ״ (Exodus 9:14) — to say there is no other authority besides Him, blessed be He, which implies that Pharaoh claimed there is yet another god. And it appears that he made this claim regarding the constellation Aries (טָלֶה) and the sun, for we find that three plagues prevented the light of the sun and the stars. For when the hail descended, surely the heavens were overcast with clouds so that they could not see the sun during the entire time the hail was falling. And in the plague of the locusts it is written ״וַיְכַס אֶת עֵין הָאָרֶץ״ (Exodus 10:15) — that is, the sun, for it is the eye of the earth; therefore ״וַתֶּחְשַׁךְ הָאָרֶץ״ (ibid.). And likewise in the plague of darkness, and likewise the plague of the firstborn at night. And all this is proof that Pharaoh claimed regarding the sun and the constellation Aries that their power is also great; therefore it is stated of the hail ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא הָיָה כָמֹהוּ״ (Exodus 9:18), and likewise of the locusts it is stated ״לְפָנָיו לֹא הָיָה אַרְבֶּה כָּמֹהוּ״ (Exodus 10:14). And all this is a sign for the statement ״כִּי אֵין כָּמֹנִי בְּכָל הָאָרֶץ״. And all the more so the plague of the firstborn, which is a sign for the humbling of the constellation Aries, firstborn among all the constellations. And this matter is clearer than the explanation of Mahari'a, and the language of the verse proves it — the one that says ״כִּי אֵין כָּמֹנִי״." "<b>Behold, I will strike with the staff, etc.</b> Regarding the ten utterances of these ten plagues, opinions abound as to why and on what account these plagues came upon them, and all the commentators went out to gather reasons, each according to his own understanding. I too will not withhold my mouth from bringing ideas close to the plain sense of the verses." "And behold, the plague of the Nile came for the sin that Pharaoh said, ״לִי יְאֹרִי וַאֲנִי עֲשִׂיתִנִי״ (Ezekiel 29:3), and for the sin of casting the children into the Nile. And the death of the fish was because they sought to nullify from Israel the blessing of ״וְיִדְגּוּ לָרֹב בְּקֶרֶב הָאָרֶץ״ (Genesis 48:16)." "Frogs came because he denied HaShem and said, לֹא יָדַעְתִּי אֶת ה׳; therefore the frogs came, who knew HaShem and surrendered their lives for the sanctification of His name, blessed be He, as stated above. And the Nile swarmed with frogs, for the admission of a litigant is like a hundred witnesses — for they said that the Nile has an aspect of divinity." "Lice came upon them because every man engaged in hard labor is full of lice that come from the sweat produced through exertion and the heating of the body. And the reason the dust of the earth became lice is that all their labor was in the field, that is, in the dust of the earth, and it is what causes them sweat and lice; therefore the dust of the earth brought lice also upon the Egyptians." "Arov (wild beasts) came upon them because they enslaved Israel, who were compared to beasts, as Rashi explained on the verse ״כִּי חָיוֹת הֵנָּה״ (Exodus 1:19); and in all the land of Goshen there was no arov, for no beast harms its fellow." "Pestilence among the livestock of the Egyptians came because the tribes were men of livestock when they came to Egypt, and now they had exchanged their trade for labor in mortar and bricks; and consequently their own livestock was ruined. Therefore ״וַיָּמָת כֹּל מִקְנֵה מִצְרָיִם״ (Exodus 9:6)." "Disease came upon them, because they separated their wives from their husbands; therefore they were struck with disease that makes marital relations difficult, as it is written (Genesis 12:17) ״וַיְנַגַּע ה׳ אֶת פַּרְעֹה״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory explained (Genesis Rabbah 41:2) that he was stricken with a disease that makes marital relations difficult, and this was for Pharaoh like a sign of what was to come." "Hail and sounds — HaShem made His voice heard in his ear, because he sinned with the voice and said ״מִי ה׳ אֲשֶׁר אֶשְׁמַע בְּקֹלוֹ״ (Exodus 5:2). And since he did not want to hearken to the voice of HaShem, therefore HaShem made him hear His voice against his will — ״קוֹל ה׳ עַל הַמָּיִם, קוֹל ה׳ חֹצֵב לַהֲבוֹת אֵשׁ״ (Psalms 29:3, 7) — that traversed the land. To say: if of your own good will you did not want to hearken to the voice of HaShem, now hear His voice — ״קוֹל ה׳ יָחִיל״ (Psalms 29:8) — Pharaoh and all his host." "Locusts came upon them, because they sought to annul from Israel ״וְהַרְבָּה אַרְבֶּה אֶת זַרְעֲךָ״ (Genesis 22:17), and they said ״פֶּן יִרְבֶּה״ (Exodus 1:10); therefore He sent locusts upon them, for the word אַרְבֶּה denotes increase, as it is written (Judges 6:5) ״וּבָאוּ כְדֵי אַרְבֶּה לָרֹב״, and it is written (Jeremiah 46:23) ״כִּי רַבּוּ מֵאַרְבֶּה״." "Darkness came upon them, because they caused Israel to hide their children in the darkness, just as they hid Moses; therefore HaShem seated them in darkness. And likewise the midwives did all their deeds in darkness, and likewise Israel were swallowed into the ground in the darkness, as is stated in tractate Sotah (11a)." "The plague of the firstborn came upon them, because they sought to destroy the firstborn son of Israel. But the waters of the sea — HaShem did not bring upon them; rather they themselves, with the willfulness of their own hearts, ran toward it, as will be explained with HaShem's help at the verse ״כִּי בַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר זָדוּ עֲלֵיהֶם״ (Exodus 18:11)."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:57.470248261Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}